# Shell and Python scripts run under a POSIX shell or interpreter, which reads a CR as part of a word: a checkout that
# turned their line ends into CRLF (core.autocrlf on Windows) makes every one of them fail with "Syntax error: word
# unexpected". Kept as LF whatever the machine, so the same tree builds in Git Bash, WSL and on the radio.
*.sh		text eol=lf
*.py		text eol=lf
Makefile	text eol=lf
*.mk		text eol=lf

# The site's JavaScript: its tests read these files as text and match exact source lines (site/tests/buildfit.test.mjs
# and the rest), so a CRLF checkout fails them on a machine where nothing is wrong
*.js		text eol=lf
*.mjs		text eol=lf
*.json		text eol=lf

# The test vectors: the host tests compare them byte for byte with what the generator prints (tests/host/Makefile
# run-dstar), and the generators print LF
tests/vectors/*	text eol=lf

# Everything else may take the platform's line ends: the editors here write CRLF and nothing in the build reads those
# files a line at a time
*		text=auto
